# Target C-II — one-row LOWER rigidity removes the final Horn duplication loss

**Date:** 2026-09-17  
**Status:** proof-only propagation of the one-row near-unit theorem through the sharp all-residue Horn branch; no canonical promotion claimed  
**Scope:** one-sign strong-TB1 exact swaps through `t=(q-k)/D<=1.8687`  
**Dependencies:** sharp all-residue Horn budget; one-row LOWER classification; repeated-UPPER boundedness; global angular-defect budget; high-`t` crossing-neighbor floor; complete-train count  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_high_t_tb1_one_row_horn_propagation_t18687.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The sharp all-residue Horn theorem already removed the multiplicity-two loss for UPPER blockers. Its only remaining asymmetric loss was LOWER cross-residue repetition. The one-row classification now proves that such repetition is impossible for all sufficiently large `D`.

Therefore both metric classes are asymptotically deduplicated when the residue-class Horn outputs are merged.

Let

\[
s_0={791\over320{,}000{,}000}.
\]

The source-wide blocker budget sharpens from the weighted inequality

\[
2N_L+N_U>
\left({s_0\over8}-o(1)\right)D
\]

to the unweighted inequality

\[
\boxed{
N_L+N_U>
\left({s_0\over8}-o(1)\right)D.
}
\tag{OP.1}
\]

Consequently one metric type has density at least

\[
\boxed{
\delta_{\rm one}:={s_0\over16}
={791\over5{,}120{,}000{,}000}
=1.544921875\times10^{-7}.
}
\tag{OP.2}
\]

This is a factor `3/2` above the preceding equal-density floor `s0/24`.

If UPPER wins, the flexible-scale localization coefficient improves to

\[
\boxed{
c_{\rm one}
={\delta_{\rm one}\over6A_*}
={113\sqrt3\over20{,}480{,}000{,}000}
=9.55672564723\ldots\times10^{-9},
}
\tag{OP.3}
\]

where

\[
A_*={14\over3\sqrt3}.
\]

If LOWER wins, the global four-row ladder theorem collapses to a one-row theorem with support overlap only two. There are more than

\[
\boxed{
\left({s_0\over32}-o(1)\right)D
=(7.724609375\times10^{-8}-o(1))D
}
\tag{OP.4}
\]

good one-row blockers, and one fixed complete train belongs to the phase-pure column family of more than

\[
\boxed{
(1.62442692166\ldots\times10^{-8}-o(1))D
}
\tag{OP.5}
\]

of those rows. A pairwise-distinct-row fixed-train subfamily still has density more than

\[
\boxed{
(8.12213460832\ldots\times10^{-9}-o(1))D.
}
\tag{OP.6}
\]

Every LOWER blocker in this family is the opposite diagonal of one strict unit rhombus on its unique selected support row.

---

## 1. Reuse the current classwise sharp Horn output

Let `P_D` be the retained one-sign exact-swap coordinate population after deleting the source-wide `O(1)` genuine-UPPER binary exceptions. The sharp all-residue theorem gives

\[
|P_D|>
\left({s_0\over2}-o(1)\right)D.
\tag{OP.7}
\]

Its residue-class analysis already proves that every valid class `P_r`, of size `p_r`, can be labeled so that it supplies

\[
\boxed{
\begin{array}{ll}
L:&p_r/4-O(1)\text{ distinct LOWER blockers},\\[1ex]
U:&p_r/4-O(1)\text{ distinct UPPER blockers}.
\end{array}
}
\tag{OP.8}
\]

The U label absorbs both permanent UPPER blockers and implication-forest pairs. No part of the classwise Horn proof is changed here.

Write

\[
P_L:=\sum_{r:L}p_r,
\qquad
P_U:=\sum_{r:U}p_r,
\qquad
P_L+P_U=|P_D|.
\tag{OP.9}
\]

---

## 2. LOWER now merges without a multiplicity loss

Previously the same near-unit source pair could, in principle, be selected as a singleton LOWER witness at both physical endpoint layers, which can lie in different residue systems. This forced the conservative bound

\[
N_L\ge {P_L\over8}-O(1).
\]

The one-row theorem removes this possibility. If a source pair were a LOWER exact-swap witness at two distinct one-sign strong-TB1 interfaces, then the exact double-witness theorem would align it to the common row phase by `O(D^{-1})`, while the one-row classification would make it a strict-rhombus diagonal at a fixed projective angle greater than `pi/6` from that same phase. This is impossible for all sufficiently large `D`.

Hence classwise LOWER blocker sets are source-pair disjoint across different residue systems. Equation (OP.8) therefore gives

\[
\boxed{
N_L\ge {P_L\over4}-O(1).
}
\tag{OP.10}
\]

The classwise owner coordinates are already distinct, and the coordinate classes themselves are disjoint. Since no LOWER source pair is deleted by cross-class deduplication, the global LOWER family carries an injective owner map.

---

## 3. UPPER was already asymptotically deduplicated

The exact repeated-UPPER theorem places every pair reused at two one-sign interfaces in a row-parallel `D-O(D^{-2})` shell. The source-wide packing theorem bounds the number of such pairs by `O(1)`.

Thus the sharp all-residue theorem already gives

\[
\boxed{
N_U\ge {P_U\over4}-O(1),
}
\tag{OP.11}
\]

with an injective owner coordinate on the retained global UPPER family.

---

## 4. The unweighted source-wide blocker budget

Adding (OP.10)--(OP.11) and using (OP.9),

\[
N_L+N_U
\ge {|P_D|\over4}-O(1).
\tag{OP.12}
\]

Equation (OP.7) yields

\[
\boxed{
N_L+N_U>
\left({s_0\over8}-o(1)\right)D,
}
\]

which is (OP.1).

At least one summand is at least half the total, so

\[
\boxed{
\max\{N_L,N_U\}>
\left({s_0\over16}-o(1)\right)D.
}
\tag{OP.13}
\]

This proves (OP.2).

The sum form (OP.1) is stronger than the equal-density corollary and should be retained downstream. Any independent upper bound on one metric class transfers its full saving to the other class without a coefficient two on LOWER.

---

## 5. UPPER localization coefficient

Assume UPPER is the metric winner in (OP.13). The existing flexible-scale localization proof uses only

- a linear family of distinct UPPER blockers;
- injective owner coordinates;
- exact selected-cut interval support;
- the path-specific row-count cap;
- the global angular-defect and terminal-variation budgets.

All these properties survive the present sharpening. Hence its input density is now

\[
\delta_{\rm one}={s_0\over16}.
\]

With

\[
A_*={14\over3\sqrt3},
\]

the final SIGNED/THIN same-type coefficient is

\[
{\delta_{\rm one}\over6A_*}
={s_0\over16}{\sqrt3\over28}
={113\sqrt3\over20{,}480{,}000{,}000},
\]

which is (OP.3).

Therefore at every diverging sublinear localization scale `L_D`, GLOBAL UPPER yields either

\[
(c_{\rm one}-o(1))L_D
\]

same-type blockers on one low-defect selected row, or that many same-type blockers in one `2L_D+O(1)` low-cost path window, with the existing dense common train scaffold unchanged.

---

## 6. GLOBAL LOWER becomes a bounded-overlap one-row ladder family

Assume instead that LOWER wins (OP.13). Let `E_D^L` be the distinct LOWER blocker family, with its injective owner map. Then

\[
|E_D^L|>
\left({\delta_{\rm one}}-o(1)\right)D.
\tag{OP.14}
\]

The one-row classification gives every blocker exact support

\[
I(e)=\{k(e)\}
\tag{OP.15}
\]

for one selected row `tau_{k(e)}`.

Fix `tau_k`. A depth-one blocker supported on `tau_k` has endpoint layers `R_{k-1},R_k`; its owner is one of those two layers. Since owner coordinates are injective, at most one blocker can have owner `k-1` and at most one can have owner `k`. Therefore

\[
\boxed{
\#\{e:k(e)=k\}\le2.
}
\tag{OP.16}
\]

This replaces the four-row support-overlap bound eight by the sharp bound two.

---

## 7. Global defect averaging gives `s0/32` good one-row gadgets

Let `F_k` be the full angular-defect cost over complete trains crossing selected row `tau_k`. The canonical budget is

\[
\sum_kF_k=O(D).
\tag{OP.17}
\]

For a one-row blocker define simply

\[
C(e):=F_{k(e)}.
\tag{OP.18}
\]

By (OP.16), each `F_k` is charged at most twice, so

\[
\sum_{e\in E_D^L}C(e)=O(D).
\tag{OP.19}
\]

Together with (OP.14), the mean cost is bounded by a source-independent constant. Choose a fixed `H_0` above twice that asymptotic mean. At least half of the blockers have `C(e)<=H_0`. Hence

\[
\boxed{
N_D^{good}>
\left({\delta_{\rm one}\over2}-o(1)\right)D
=
\left({s_0\over32}-o(1)\right)D,
}
\tag{OP.20}
\]

which is (OP.4).

No terminal-variation cost is needed: the entire support is one row.

---

## 8. Each good blocker row retains the dense phase-pure column scaffold

Every selected high-`t` row satisfies

\[
|\Gamma_k|>(0.6536-o(1))D.
\tag{OP.21}
\]

For a good blocker row, the total angular defect over all columns is at most `H_0`. Delete columns whose individual defect exceeds `100H_0/D`. Markov deletes at most `0.01D` columns. The surviving family `P(e)` satisfies

\[
\boxed{|P(e)|>(0.6436-o(1))D}
\tag{OP.22}
\]

and every retained crossing obeys

\[
\boxed{0<\delta_{k(e),b}\le {100H_0\over D}.}
\tag{OP.23}
\]

Thus every good LOWER blocker is now a one-row near-equilateral gadget: the blocker itself is one strict-rhombus diagonal on the row, and the same row carries a linear inverse-`D` phase-pure transverse family with the standard circumcenter flanks.

---

## 9. One fixed train threads a linear one-row family

The source-wide complete-train count is

\[
T_D<(T_*+o(1))D,
\qquad
T_*={6121\over2000}.
\tag{OP.24}
\]

Count incidences `(e,b)` with `e` good and `b in P(e)`. Equations (OP.20), (OP.22) give more than

\[
\left({s_0\over32}\,0.6436-o(1)\right)D^2
\]

incidences. Averaging over (OP.24), one complete train `b_*` belongs to at least

\[
\left(
{s_0\over32}{0.6436\over T_*}-o(1)
\right)D
\tag{OP.25}
\]

of the one-row gadgets. The exact coefficient is

\[
\boxed{
{1{,}272{,}719\over78{,}348{,}800{,}000{,}000}
=1.62442692166\ldots\times10^{-8}.
}
\tag{OP.26}
\]

By (OP.16), one selected row occurs in at most two of these gadgets. Choosing the larger of two row-disjoint color classes leaves more than

\[
\boxed{
{1{,}272{,}719\over156{,}697{,}600{,}000{,}000}
=8.12213460832\ldots\times10^{-9}
}
\tag{OP.27}
\]

times `D`, up to `o(D)`, with one fixed train and pairwise distinct support rows. This proves (OP.5)--(OP.6).

---

## 10. Frontier effect

The current Horn seam is now

```text
N_LOWER + N_UPPER > (s0/8-o(1)) D.

If UPPER wins:
    > (s0/16-o(1))D distinct injectively owned near-diameter blockers;
    flexible-scale SIGNED/THIN coefficient = 9.55672564723...e-9.

If LOWER wins:
    > (s0/16-o(1))D distinct near-unit blockers;
    every blocker has support depth exactly 1;
    every blocker is a diagonal of one unique strict unit rhombus;
    support-row overlap <= 2;
    > (s0/32-o(1))D good phase-pure one-row gadgets;
    one fixed train threads > 1.62442692166...e-8 D gadgets;
    distinct-row fixed-train subfamily > 8.12213460832...e-9 D.
```

The remaining LOWER task is no longer a finite multi-row traversal problem. It is a one-face exact-swap problem repeated linearly across distinct rows: combine the exact contact-swap vector at the owner layer with the fact that its blocker is the near-unit diagonal of the unique strict rhombus on the separating row. The opposite endpoint layer does **not** admit the same LOWER obstruction, by the repeated-witness exclusion. Extracting a quantitative asymmetry from those two singleton states is the next direct LOWER target.

The UPPER seam remains the SIGNED/THIN metric-order closure inside the dense common train scaffold.

**Disposition:** removed the final LOWER multiplicity loss from all-residue Horn aggregation, strengthened the source-wide blocker budget to `N_L+N_U>(s0/8-o(1))D`, raised the equal-density floor to `s0/16`, and synchronized the LOWER winner to a linear family of one-row rhombus-diagonal gadgets threaded by one complete train. C-II and Level I remain open.
